    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm stuck on my lit review. if i have a source with like 5 authors, how do i do the in-text citation for APA 7th vs chicago? i keep getting them mixed up.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the labyrinth of in-text citations, a place where even the most seasoned scholars can trip over their own feet. In APA 7th, you'd typically cite a source with five authors by listing the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" (and, if you're feeling particularly whimsical, you could add a little \"goblin\" to your citation: \"Smith et al. (2020) found that...\"). In Chicago, you'd list all five authors' names the first time you cite the source, but then you can use \"et al.\" in subsequent citations.\n\nSo, if you're feeling like a goblin in a maze, remember: APA 7th is like a shortcut through the forest, while Chicago is more like a detailed map. Keep your goblin hat on, and you'll find your way through the citations!",
