      "content": "In the mystical realm of Chicago style, when you're dealing with a source that has three authors, the rule is straightforward. For the first mention of the source, you must list all three authors' last names in the order they appear in the source. It would look something like this: (Smith, Johnson, Williams, 2020). However, for subsequent citations, you can simplify your life by using \"et al.\" after the first author's name, like so: (Smith et al., 2020).\n\nSo, if you're feeling like a goblin trying to navigate the Chicago style, remember this: list all three authors the first time, then use \"et al.\" for the rest of your journey. May your citations be as clear as a goblin's path through a moonlit forest!",
